lesson: 241
title: Paying with US coins
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: In the United States a dollar is worth 100 cents, and a quarter is worth 25 cents, so 4 quarters make a dollar.
explain: To find the change in cents, turn the money paid into cents, find the cost of all the things in cents, and take the cost away.
explain: Paying 1 dollar for 2 apples at 45 cents each: the apples cost 90 cents, and 100 - 90 = 10 cents come back.
vars: a=name b=name d=2..5 k=2..6 c=3..19 z=2..9
let: p = c * 5
let: cost = k * p
need: cost < d * 100
answer: d * 100 - cost
exercise: {a} buys {k} apples at {p} cents each and pays with {d} dollars. How many cents of change does {a.he} get?
exercise: A pencil costs {p} cents. {a} buys {k} pencils and hands over {d} dollars. How many cents come back?
exercise: {a} has {d*4} quarters. {a.He} spends some of them on {k} stickers at {p} cents each. How many cents does {a.he} have left?
exercise: At a school fair in Texas, cookies cost {p} cents each. {a} buys {k} cookies with {d} dollars. How much change, in cents, does {a.he} receive?
exercise: {a} pays for {k} erasers costing {p} cents apiece with {d} one-dollar bills. How many cents of change are given?
exercise: {a} empties {a.his} piggy bank and finds {d*4} quarters. {a.He} buys {k} postcards at {p} cents each. How many cents remain?
exercise: {k} lollipops at {p} cents each, paid with {d} bucks. how many cents change
exercise: {a} got {d} dollars, buys {k} gumballs at {p+5} — no wait, {p} cents each. change in cents?
exercise: {a} grabbed {k} bananas {p} cents each, the line was soooo long lol, paid {d} dollars. how many cents back
exercise: {a} paid in quarters, had {d*4} of em, bought {k} bouncy balls at {p} cents a ball. cents left?
exercise: {b} says the change is {d*100-p} cents. but {a} bought {k} pens at {p} cents each with {d} dollars. what's the real change in cents
test: How many cents should {a} get back after spending {d} dollars on {k} juice boxes priced at {p} cents apiece?
test: {k} stamps cost {p} cents each. {a} gives the clerk {d} dollar bills. What change in cents does the clerk hand back?
test: With {d*4} quarters in {a.his} pocket, {a} buys {k} bags of chips for {p} cents a bag. What does {a.he} still have, in cents?
test: {a} buys {k} oranges at {p} cents each and pays {d} dollars; {b} buys a hat for {z} dollars. How many cents of change does {a} get?
test: yo {a} paid {d} dollars for {k} candy bars, {p} cents a piece. how much change in cents
test: {a}'s got {d*4} quarters, wants {k} pretzels at {p} cents each. how many cents left after
test: my bro says {k} muffins at {p} cents off {d} dollars leaves {d*100-cost+5} cents. right? how many cents really
exam: Determine, in cents, the change due to {a}, who tenders {d.words} dollars in payment for {k.words} articles priced at {p} cents per article.
exam: It is given that a purchaser in possession of {d*4} quarters acquires {k} items at a unit price of {p} cents. Ascertain the sum, expressed in cents, remaining thereafter.
exam: A payment of {d} dollars having been made in respect of {k} pencils, each priced at {p} cents, state the quantity of cents to be returned to the purchaser.
exam: Whereas {a} remits {d} dollars and subsequently receives {k} apples valued at {p} cents apiece, compute the balance owed to {a.him}, in cents.
exercise: Since Mom got laid off, {a} counts every cent. {a.He} buys {k} apples at {p} cents each and pays with {d} dollars. How many cents of change come back?
exercise: {b} reckons the shopkeeper makes little mistakes with the change, yeah right, he's a crook. {a} buys {k} donuts at {p} cents each and hands over {d} dollars. how many cents should come back, so we can catch the old sod out?
test: {a}'s dad is between jobs, so {a} pays for {k} snacks at {p} cents apiece with {d} dollar bills. What change, in cents, should {a.he} receive?
